<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bakenranef</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 720 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Bocchoris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bakenranef, called Bocchoris by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, was the second and last king of the Twenty-fourth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a> in the western Delta in the 720s BCE. Greek writers remembered him as a wise lawgiver who reformed contract law. Around 720 BCE the Kushite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabaka">Shabaka</a> conquered the Delta, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> he burned Bakenranef alive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bakenranef.jpg" alt="Bakenranef" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Auguste Mariette (1821-1881) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apis_Bakenranef_6_Mariette.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
